course: dictionary
lesson: 4927
title: Impasto: paint you can feel
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: In an art lesson you may meet the word impasto. It is a word for a way of painting, not for a picture. The clues are in the words around it, so read them closely.
explain: «Very thick paint that sticks out from the canvas is called impasto.» The clues are thick and sticks out. Impasto paint stands up from the surface, so you can see the brush or knife marks in it.
explain: The sentence also says impasto can be done with oil paint or with acrylic, «which dries much faster». So impasto is about how thick the paint is, not which kind of paint you use. To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence: "Very thick paint that sticks out from the canvas is called thick paint" still makes sense, so you are on the right track.
fact: Impasto means very thick paint that stands up from the surface of the painting.
fact: You can often see the brush marks or knife marks in impasto paint.
fact: Impasto can be done with oil paint or with acrylic paint.
fact: Acrylic paint dries much faster than oil paint, as the example sentence tells us.
fact: Van Gogh used impasto in The Starry Night and Sunflowers. Phrases to know: impasto technique, thick impasto.
exercise: «Very thick paint that sticks out from the canvas is called impasto.» What is impasto? | Thin, see-through paint that you can barely see | *A way of painting with very thick paint | A type of paper for drawing | A wooden frame for a picture
exercise: Which painter used impasto in The Starry Night? | Claude Monet | Leonardo da Vinci | *Van Gogh | Pablo Picasso
exercise: The sentence says acrylic «dries much faster». Which paint is this about? | Chalk | Oil paint | Clay | *Acrylic
test: What can you often see on an impasto painting? | A thin frame around the edge | *Brush or knife marks standing up from the surface | A smooth, flat surface with no marks at all | Paint so thin you can see the paper through it
test: Which of these best matches the phrase thick impasto? | Paint washed on very thinly | A pencil drawing with light lines | Paper cut into shapes | *Paint laid on very thickly
exam: Why might an artist pick acrylic for impasto, according to the sentence? | Because it is a type of pencil | Because it is always black | Because it makes paintings smaller | *Because it dries much faster
